'''''Yucca gloriosa'' var. ''tristis''''' (syn. '''''Yucca recurvifolia''''', ''Yucca gloriosa'' var. ''recurvifolia''),<ref name=WCSP>{{Citation |contribution=Yucca recurvifolia |title=World Checklist of Selected Plant Families |publisher=The Board of Trustees of the [[Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew]] |url=http://apps.kew.org/wcsp/namedetail.do?name_id=291729 |access-date=2012-02-27}}, search for "Yucca recurvifolia"</ref> known as '''curve-leaf yucca''',<ref>{{cite usda plants|symbol=YURE2|title=''Yucca recurvifolia'' Salisb.|access-date=30 March 2026}}</ref> '''curved-leaved Spanish dagger'''<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=30 March 2026}}</ref> '''weeping yucca'''<ref name="NCEGPT">{{cite web |url=https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/yucca-gloriosa-var-tristis/ |title=''Yucca gloriosa'' var. ''tristis'' |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=<!--Not stated--> |website=North Carolina Extension Gardener Plant Toolbox |publisher=N. C. Cooperative Extension |access-date=30 March 2026 }}</ref> or '''pendulous yucca''',<ref name="NCEGPT"/> is a variety of ''[[Yucca gloriosa]]''. It is often grown as an [[ornamental plant]],<ref>{{cite book | author=Wood, John | author-link=John George Wood | title=[[Hardy Perennials and Old Fashioned Flowers]] | year=2006 |orig-year=1884 |publisher=[[Project Gutenberg|Project Gutenberg Literary Archive Foundation]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://cals.arizona.edu/pima/gardening/aridplants/Yucca_recurvifolia.html |title=''Yucca recurvifolia'' at University of Arizona |access-date=2007-03-26 |archive-date=2004-09-07 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040907050955/http://cals.arizona.edu/pima/gardening/aridplants/Yucca_recurvifolia.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> and is native to the [[Southern United States]], from coastal southeastern [[Virginia]] south through [[Florida]] and west to [[Texas]].<ref>{{cite book | author=Irish, Gary & Mary | title = Agaves, Yuccas, and Related Plants | url=https://archive.org/details/agavesyuccasrela0000iris | url-access=registration | year=2000| isbn = 9780881924428 }}</ref> In contrast to ''Y. gloriosa'' var. ''tristis'', the leaves of ''Y. gloriosa'' var. ''gloriosa'' are hard stiff, erect and narrower.{{Citation needed|date=February 2012}}

''Yucca gloriosa'' var. ''tristis'' is often found in sandy habits like coastal sand dunes and beach scrub along with species of ''[[Opuntia]]''.{{citation needed|date=May 2018}} Growing a trunk often {{convert|4|to|6|ft|m|1|order=flip|abbr=off}} high, this yucca will often branch and sucker to form colonies in the area it is planted in. Cultivated in the warmer areas of [[Europe]] and the parts of [[Australia]]. In the United States a popular landscape plant in beach resort areas along the lower East Coast from coastal [[Maryland]] to [[Florida]]. It (or its cultivar(s)) is a recipient of the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]] under the ''Yucca recurvifolia'' name.{{citation needed|date=August 2019}}

